The paper offers a new, fine=E2=80=91grained approach to analyzing negative=
 user reviews by splitting them into two distinct sub=E2=80=91classes: rage=
 and reason. Most existing sentiment analysis research works treat =E2=80=
=9Cnegative=E2=80=9D as a single, uniform category.
A custom annotated dataset for this fine-grained emotional classification w=
as used. However, the dataset collected during the research was limited to =
conatin only negative reviews. It is not clear what would be the preformanc=
e of the method while executing on the dataset containing both positive and=
 negative reviews. (What would be the performance metrics of the classifica=
tion with the increased number of classes - better matching real-world scen=
arios?; Can the selected reviews be uniqely assigned to one the assumed cla=
sses/subclasses?)
How the original dataset was split into training, validation and testing su=
bsets? How the performance of the method was evaluated (which part of the o=
riginal dataset was used)? ("The labelled dataset was divided into training=
